  {
   "type": "board",
   "board": "minircbench-wiring",
   "say": [
    {
     "at": 0.5,
     "text": "The wiring, seen from the rear of the display board. All parts lie flat here. In the case they sit behind the board."
    },
    {
     "at": 9.99,
     "text": "Step 3. The boost board takes its input from the battery socket. The print beside the socket marks plus and minus: plus is the pin next to the R-T-C socket."
    },
    {
     "at": 22.45,
     "text": "Solder a red wire from the plus pin to I-N plus, and a black wire from the minus pin to I-N minus. The pins are 1.25 millimetres apart: use thin wire and little solder. The cell stays unplugged."
    },
    {
     "at": 37.72,
     "text": "The cell then feeds the board and the boost board, and the board's charger charges it over U-S-B. No fuse is fitted: a short on these two wires is a short on the cell."
    },
    {
     "at": 50.99,
     "text": "Step 4. The enable pad. Solder a 10 kilo ohm resistor from the enable pad to I-N minus. It holds the 5 volts off while the board is off or the wire is loose."
    },
    {
     "at": 63.52,
     "text": "Then plug the 12 pin lead into the board. Its eleventh wire is I-O 28. Solder it to the enable pad. The bench switches the servo supply with this pin."
    },
    {
     "at": 75.53,
     "text": "Step 5. The current sensor goes on the I 2 C socket, with the 4 pin lead. Ground to G-N-D, 3.3 volts to V-C-C, S-D-A to S-D-A, S-C-L to S-C-L."
    },
    {
     "at": 86.95,
     "text": "The sensor runs on 3.3 volts, never on 5: its pull-ups would lift the touch controller's bus to 5 volts. Do not use a ready made Qwiic cable; its pin order is different."
    },
    {
     "at": 100.37,
     "text": "Step 6. The 5 volt path. First, two parts across the output of the boost board. A 5.6 volt zener diode, rated 2 to 5 watts, from out plus to out minus, its band on out plus. It clamps the output if the voltage rises."
    },
    {
     "at": 117.27,
     "text": "And an electrolytic capacitor of 470 micro farad, rated 10 volts or more, as a buffer. Its plus lead goes to out plus. The stripe on the can marks minus."
    },
    {
     "at": 129.5,
     "text": "Then out plus goes on to V-in plus on the sensor, at the two holes of the screw terminal, not at the header."
    },
    {
     "at": 138.4,
     "text": "From V-in minus, the measured 5 volts go to the middle pin of both servo connectors. Out minus goes to the minus pin of both."
    },
    {
     "at": 148.56,
     "text": "Step 7. The signals. Wire 9 of the 12 pin lead is T-X-D, G-P-I-O 0, the bench's S-I-G pin. It goes to the signal pin of the E-S-C connector."
    },
    {
     "at": 159.83,
     "text": "Wire 10 is R-X-D, G-P-I-O 1, the A-U-X pin. It goes to the signal pin of the T-M-T connector."
    },
    {
     "at": 167.62,
     "text": "Nine wires of this lead are not used: both grounds, V-bus, the two U-S-B data wires, 3.3 volts, S-D-A, S-C-L and I-O 29. Take them out of the plug, so no bare end can touch a part."
    },
    {
     "at": 181.85,
     "text": "Each contact is held by a small plastic latch of the housing. Lift the latch a little with a needle, and pull the wire out to the back, without force. A latch bent too far breaks off."
    },
    {
     "at": 196.31,
     "text": "Step 8. Check before the cell goes in. With a meter: no connection between plus and minus at the servo connectors, and none between the battery pins."
    },
    {
     "at": 208.25,
     "text": "Then plug the cell into the battery socket. The 5 volts must stay off: measure 0 volts between plus and minus at both servo connectors. If you read more, do not plug in a servo."
    }
   ],
